\f1\fs22 To the lab in the Pale, Jamison Hardy sent a minion, one Arjen Jansen.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Hardy did not call Jansen a minion. Hardy rarely called anybody anything. Names encouraged the sort of familiarity that eventually led to Christmas cards.\
The lift took long enough for Jansen to adjust his green tie twice. At the bottom, it slowed aggressively enough to make him glad he\'92d finally had that knee surgery. The doors opened abruptly enough that Jansen felt as if the lift wanted him out.\
A woman was waiting on the other side. \'93Arjen Jansen?\'94\
\'93Yes.\'94\
\'93Celeste d\'92Vane.\'94 Celeste wore no lab coat. She wouldn\'92t; her wardrobe was expensive without making an issue of it, and it needed to be seen. She carried a tablet under one arm in a case that accessorised as much as it protected.\
Jansen waited.\
Celeste waited too, but with considerably more enjoyment. Eventually she said, \'93Executive Liaison,\'94 then turned and started walking.\
Jansen, who would never be hurried, followed at his own pace, while Celeste set off as if the building belonged to somebody she knew socially.\
Dr Hamish Hermann occupied a room that appeared to be half laboratory, half office and half boarding-school lodging, a calculation nobody had seen fit to challenge. Books lay open on chairs. Two coffee mugs might have been experiments in progress. A shirt hung from the back of a cupboard door. Through a broad glass wall beyond him, the actual laboratory shone with the merciless hygiene of a place where even the dust had security clearance.\
Hermann himself stood at a raised desk entering figures into a terminal while three incubators blinked at him.\
He looked up.\
\'93Mr Jansen, here to see us,\'94 Celeste announced.\
\'93Excellent.\'94 Hermann came around the desk with his hand extended, changed his mind when he noticed the gloves, removed one glove, discovered his hand was damp, wiped it on his trousers and tried again.\
Jansen looked at the hand until Hermann put it away.\
Celeste settled onto a stool in the corner. \'93Mr Jansen wants a technical briefing.\'94 She looked at Jansen. \'93Don\'92t you, Arjen.\'94 It wasn\'92t a question.\
Jansen glared at her.\
\'93What?\'94 That was a question, and Celeste affected offence at having to ask it. \'93I\'92m liaising. Executively.\'94\
Jansen suspected d\'92Vane\'92s skills were more in the line of disarmament than liaison.\
\'93You will tell me about TPlusVirus.\'94\
\'93Um. Sure.\'94 Hermann thought and brightened. \'93To understand TPlus you must first understand TMinus. You see, it\'92s an engineered lentiviral vector built to recognise selected genetic markers. SNPs, haplotype blocks, inherited phenotypic signatures. In a person without the nominated profile it could sit quietly, pass unnoticed and move on. In a person with the profile, the integrase unlocks the payload.\'94\
\'93Explain payload,\'94 Jansen said as Celeste admired his lack of social skills.\
\'93Cytotoxic,\'94 Hermann answered.\
\'93How cytotoxic?\'94\
\'93Very.\'94\
Jansen looked through the glass at the bright cleanroom.\
\'93Acute systemic failure,\'94 Hermann explained. \'93Depending on the target architecture, aneurysm, fulminant organ collapse, occasionally what presents as an inherited catastrophic event, you see.\'94\
\'93You make it look like a natural death.\'94\
\'93A highly engineered death,\'94 Hermann said, quite satisfied with himself.\
Celeste folded her arms.\
\'93And TPlus?\'94 Jansen was not patient.\
Until then Hermann had merely been a scientist discussing work. Now he became a father being asked whether anybody would like to see photographs.\
\'93TPlus is what happens when you take the delivery architecture of TMinus and reverse the intent.\'94\
\'93You stopped it killing people.\'94\
\'93We made killing people look embarrassingly primitive.\'94\
\'93Hamish has become ethical,\'94 Celeste said. \'93It\'92s been very difficult for everyone.\'94\
Hermann gave her a brief look and continued. \'93Rather than carrying the cytotoxic payload, it carries a collection of biological improvements assembled from organisms that evolution, you see, had already subjected to otherwise unliveable conditions. Tardigrade damage-suppressor proteins protect chromatin against radiation and oxidative stress; transdifferentiation factors derived from 
\f2\i Turritopsis
\f1\i0 \'97\'94\
\'93Immortal jellyfish,\'94 Celeste injected.\
\'93Yes, quite.\'94 Hermann accelerated as he spoke. \'93Transdifferentiation factors reset cellular ageing pathways. Extremophile fungi contribute metabolic mechanisms that allow tissue to continue functioning under oxygen deprivation and other forms of stress. There are protein-expression regulators, repair cascades, mitochondrial improvements, suppression controls\'97\'94\
Celeste noticed Jansen losing interest in the data. \'93Just remember water bear, jellyfish, fire fungus.\'94\
\'93Tardigrades are not bears,\'94 Hermann objected.\
\'93Gummy bear, jellyfish, fire fungus.\'94\
\'93Explain the benefits.\'94 Jansen did not ask questions.\
\'93Everything gets better.\'94\
\'93Define everything.\'94\
\'93Even cancer becomes extraordinarily difficult to sustain because the mechanisms that ordinarily allow damaged cells to accumulate mutations are continually corrected or suppressed. Tissue repairs faster. Senescent cells are replaced. Vascular damage reverses. Organ function improves. Muscular damage heals with an efficiency we had not previously believed mammalian tissue capable of.\'94\
\'93It cures cancer,\'94 Jansen checked.\
\'93Functionally obsolete.\'94\
\'93Heart disease.\'94 Jansen sounded like he was reading off a clipboard.\
\'93Reversed, in our current models.\'94\
Jansen sucked in a deep breath and went through more ailments while Hermann answered in various ways that meant, 
\f2\i Check
\f1\i0 .\
Jansen paused.\
Celeste, who had looked contemplative, brightened. \'93Can it do anything for meetings?\'94\
Jansen did not smile. Hermann looked puzzled.\
\'93Life expectancy,\'94 Jansen said.\
Hermann nodded, glad to be past the stamp collecting. \'93With maintenance, our present expectation is a healthy functional lifespan somewhere between a hundred and forty and a hundred and sixty years.\'94\
Jansen chewed once on the inside of his cheek.\
Celeste noticed. \'93Ah,\'94 she said.\
Hermann looked at her. \'93What?\'94\
\'93That\'92s the first thing he\'92s liked.\'94\
\'93I have expressed no preference,\'94 Jansen said.\
\'93Exactly.\'94\
He turned back to Hermann.\
\'93Explain required maintenance.\'94\
Hermann became enthusiastic again. \'93The TPlus payload, unlike TMinus, does not ordinarily integrate into the chromosomes. The therapeutic material remains episomal: separate genetic rings occupying the nucleus, active but independent of the host genome.\'94\
\'93So it\'92s temporary.\'94 Jansen would not risk a question even when he was guessing.\
\'93That is offensively reductive,\'94 Hermann said.\
\'93Temporary,\'94 Jansen decided.\
Hermann went to a refrigerated cabinet, took out a rack of sealed sample vials and held one towards the light. \'93The cell is hostile to foreign material. That\'92s desirable; we exploit it. The episomal structures are gradually diluted through cellular turnover, while the engineered degradation sequence clears the remaining active proteins. Fourteen to twenty-one days and the optimisation curve begins to fall away.\'94\
Jansen looked at the vial. \'93TPlus wears off.\'94\
Hermann\'92s expression suggested that a Stradivarius had just been described as a box with strings. \'93The treatment has a designed half-life.\'94\
\'93It wears off. That\'92s good.\'94\
\'93Good for business,\'94 Celeste observed.\
Hermann was getting defensive. \'93It is controllably transient.\'94\
Celeste nodded.\
Hermann returned the vial to the rack. \'93The point is control. Permanent genomic modification would be medically reckless\'97\'94\
\'93\'97and commercially unnecessary.\'94 Celeste finished the sentence, but Hermann scowled.\
Jansen said, \'93A patient needs injections for life.\'94\
\'93At the optimal maintenance threshold, yes.\'94\
Celeste looked between them. \'93Immortality with invoices.\'94\
\'93It is not immortality,\'94 Hermann countered, and Celeste regretted her drive to be funny over being helpful. Executive Liaisoning allowed her to get away with it more often than not, though perhaps not now.\
Jansen stared through the glass again. Beyond it, technicians moved between benches carrying trays and sealed containers. None of them looked immortal.\
\'93Can it become permanent?\'94\
Hermann weighed his answer. \'93No. Not in any statistically meaningful sense.\'94\
Jansen looked back at him. \'93Explain.\'94\
Hermann folded his arms. \'93In theory, foreign episomal material 
\f2\i can
\f1\i0  become incorporated during emergency DNA repair. Illegitimate recombination. But you would require double-strand DNA breaks occurring at precisely the same time\'97the same microsecond\'97as an overwhelming local concentration of available vector, with the repair machinery sufficiently saturated to use foreign material as substrate.\'94\
\'93So it can happen.\'94\
\'93In the same sense that this building can be destroyed by a meteor.\'94\
\'93On a long enough timeline, the chance of avoiding a meteor reduces to zero,\'94 Jansen said. \'93Explain sufficiently saturated.\'94\
Hermann smiled smugly. \'93Ah, I see where you\'92re going with this. There is no safe dose that will achieve sustained integration. It\'92s lethal. The subject suffers systemic toxic shock. Catastrophic immune response. Multi-organ failure. Kidney damage of a kind I would prefer not to describe immediately before lunch.\'94\
Celeste checked the time.\
Jansen said, \'93You tested this.\'94\
Hermann stammered. \'93Well, of course\'97\'94\
\'93What he means is, we modelled it,\'94 Celeste cut in, \'93and challenged the upper range in Phase I.\'94\
\'93The safety threshold is established,\'94 Hermann said, as if that settled the matter.\
\'93Transmission,\'94 Jansen said, returning to his mental clipboard.\
\'93Technically.\'94 Hermann covered his irritation. Badly.\
Celeste made a small noise.\
Hermann ignored it. \'93The vector sheds. It can be detected in bodily fluids after treatment. That was anticipated.\'94\
\'93Airborne transmission.\'94\
\'93No.\'94\
\'93Waterborne.\'94\
\'93Not at therapeutically viable levels.\'94\
\'93Blood.\'94\
\'93Yes.\'94\
\'93Saliva.\'94\
\'93Trace.\'94\
\'93Sex.\'94\
\'93I\'92d rather not,\'94 Celeste said. \'93Not immediately before lunch.\'94\
Hermann squandered Celeste\'92s attempt to give him a moment to stop and think. \'93A partner can receive viable vector.\'94\
\'93To the full benefit of a course of injections,\'94 Jansen tested.\
Hermann made the mistake of looking pleased. \'93To a degree.\'94\
Celeste slid off the stool. \'93There\'92s a degradation curve.\'94\
Hermann nodded. \'93The transmitted vector attenuates,\'94 he said, noting that Celeste seemed mollified. Then he added, \'93Quite elegantly, actually,\'94 and wiped the smile from Celeste\'92s face.\
\'93Explain,\'94 Jansen said.\
Celeste shook her head and fiddled with her tablet.\
Hermann took a deep breath. Facts were facts; withholding them was alien to him. \'93The injected subject carries the full therapeutic load, you see. With sexual transmission, only a fraction of that viable load establishes itself in the recipient. So the secondary host receives a correspondingly reduced optimisation profile.\'94\
\'93How reduced?\'94\
\'93It varies.\'94\
Jansen waited. \'93Best case.\'94\
\'93A first-degree recipient shows substantial systemic benefit. It\'92s wonderful, really.\'94\
Celeste pressed the red button on her tablet to call security as Hermann continued. \'93Full systemic repair. Reversal of chronic disease. Substantial rejuvenation.\'94\
Jansen\'92s eyes remained on Hermann. \'93The virus will spread.\'94\
\'93Well, not as effectively as direct injection, but with further research we could improve efficacy by some margin. Imagine! No need for medical intervention; the virus will just\'85 spread.\'94 Hermann spread his hands before him and wiggled his fingers.\
Celeste looked at the floor. As deep in the ground as they were, Hermann was still digging a deeper hole. \'93The important point,\'94 she said, \'93is that it decays with each transmission.\'94\
Jansen looked at Celeste. Celeste scowled at Hermann.\
\'93What? He was going to see the data,\'94 Hermann defended.\
\'93He wasn\'92t necessarily going to enjoy it.\'94\
\'93I always enjoy data,\'94 Jansen said. He pulled his device from his pocket. He looked at Celeste, tapped something on the screen, then parked it back in his pocket. \'93And the partner can transmit it again?\'94 That almost sounded like a question.\
\'93Yes,\'94 Hermann said, \'93but this is where the attenuation becomes important.\'94 He called up a graph on a computer. \'93The first transmission loses a proportion of the original expression strength. The next loses more. Then more again. Each reproductive passage moves the vector further away from the full therapeutic phenotype.\'94\
Jansen studied the graph.\
Celeste stepped between Jansen and the screen as though the graph required physical protection. \'93The commercial point, Mr Jansen, is that the benefit degrades. The injection remains the only route to the complete product.\'94\
Jansen looked around her at Hermann.\
\'93The complete product.\'94\
\'93Yes,\'94 Hermann said. \'93Precisely. A sexually exposed subject does not receive the full lifespan extension, the complete metabolic optimisation or the predictable maintenance profile. They receive a degraded derivative.\'94\
Celeste nodded.\
\'93Like a promotional sample,\'94 Jansen said.\
Hermann looked puzzled. \'93Promotional?\'94\
\'93Mr Jansen is considering the marketing options,\'94 Celeste explained.\
Hermann considered. \'93Promotional\'85 yes, I see. I suppose so. Try the product, subscribe for full benefits.\'94\
Jansen was still studying the graph. \'93So every customer you inject becomes a distributor.\'94\
\'93I\'92m sorry?\'94 Hermann shook his head, trying to process that.\
\'93A customer could 
\f2\i subscribe
\f1\i0  to sexual delivery rather than seeing their doctor,\'94 Jansen stated.\
Celeste checked her tablet again and wondered where security was.\
\'93They are not coming,\'94 Jansen said.\
Celeste felt the chill descend from her chest as far as her knees. \'93This interview is over.\'94\
\'93This interview will continue,\'94 Jansen stated plainly, then continued with his mental clipboard. \'93A virus distributor can distribute indefinitely.\'94\
\'93At reduced efficacy,\'94 Hermann answered.\
\'93Recipients themselves become distributors.\'94\
\'93At further reduced efficacy.\'94\
\'93And so on.\'94\
\'93At increasingly trivial\'97\'94\
\'93Estimate for me how many sexual partners one might have over a lifespan of a hundred and sixty years.\'94\
\'93I\'92m only thirty-six and I\'92m bored with sex already,\'94 Celeste remarked.\
Jansen looked her up and down: at her eyebrows and fingernails, at the clothes she wore. He smelled her perfume. \'93You lie.\'94\
Hermann filled in the cold silence. \'93Personally, I still find sex marvellously efficacious.\'94\
\'93He doesn\'92t lie,\'94 Jansen said to Celeste.\
\'93He inflates budget demands,\'94 Celeste said. \'93And plagiarises.\'94\
Hermann\'92s mouth fell open.\
\'93Explain the population epidemiology,\'94 Jansen said.\
Celeste resumed her place on the stool. \'93You\'92re assuming unrestricted behaviour.\'94\
\'93I\'92m assuming customers. I\'92m assessing competition.\'94\
Hermann said, \'93Even with aggressive sexual networking, the therapeutic effect collapses with distance from the original treated host.\'94\
\'93Distance.\'94\
\'93Degrees of transmission.\'94\
\'93Project the number of sex cults that will be created.\'94\
\'93Sex cults?\'94 Hermann was appalled by the absurdity. \'93That is an offensively simplistic characterisation of the pharmacokinetics.\'94\
Even Celeste had considered that. \'93First-generation recipients of therapeutic delivery will be sought out. They will be tempted to bless their flocks.\'94\
Jansen\'92s mouth twitched.\
Celeste continued. \'93With luck, they would all get themselves killed.\'94\
Jansen drew a breath. \'93What is telling is that you have not modelled it.\'94\
Hermann was appalled. \'93You don\'92t seem to understand that we are curing disease, eliminating great suffering.\'94\
\'93It will increase great suffering. Unless the market can be controlled.\'94\
\'93We all believe in the perfection of the market, Mr Jansen,\'94 Celeste stated.\
Hermann was indignant. \'93I\'92m an atheist!\'94\
\'93It shows.\'94 Arjen Jansen set his jaw. \'93I will see the documentation now.\'94\
Celeste d\'92Vane had heard enough. Not enough to understand the science\'97Hamish Hermann had spent years making that difficult\'97but enough to recognise the particular change in temperature that occurred when a company stopped asking whether something worked and began asking where all the copies were kept.\
She left the Pale that afternoon and brought forward a wellness retreat she had been saving in case of a nervous breakdown: ten days at a volcanic mindfulness retreat. Therapeutic fasting and supervised non-attachment on an island with unreliable telecommunications were included in the package. Celeste had no intention of becoming less attached to anything, but the brochure promised massages.\
What she hadn\'92t banked on was that it would save her life.
